WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.120
Cats.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:06.470
Across the globe and throughout human history,
they’ve been worshipped, revered, feared,

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:08.210
villainized, domesticated, and cuddled.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:13.900
On the one hand they’re fluffy, floofy purring
balls of cuteness but they also act like soul-stealing

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:14.900
demons.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.870
In Japanese folklore, they mean that literally.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:22.140
Japan’s culture is rich in feline imagery
and folklore.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.710
Most often seen as protectors, defending the
home from everything from mice to bad spirits,

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:29.230
the cat is generally revered.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:31.130
Yet it is also feared.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:36.650
Believed to be in possession of powerful paranormal
abilities, they bring curses as quickly as

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:37.650
luck.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:42.269
Then there’s the Kasha, one of Japan’s
feline yōkai and a literal hellcat.

00:00:42.269 --> 00:00:48.519
This massive, snarling monster arrives in
the midst of raging thunder clouds on a mysterious

00:00:48.519 --> 00:00:53.820
floating fireball, hell-bent on stealing your
corpse from the arms of your loved ones and

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.289
dragging it to the underworld.

00:00:55.289 --> 00:00:59.649
I mean…we all know cats can be vicious, but this seems kind of extreme.

00:00:59.649 --> 00:01:04.400
Did I mention Kasha are also depicted as a
flaming chariot that transports groups of

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.540
souls to be tortured in the afterlife?

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:11.020
So is it a hellcat or a hellcart?!

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:15.310
Actually, it’s both.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.160
[Monstrum intro.]

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:23.770
Japanese Yōkai are loosely defined as monsters,
although any strange or supernatural creature

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:24.830
fits the bill.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:30.670
Kasha, the cat demons associated with transporting
corpses to their eternal damnation are certainly

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:31.670
yokai.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:36.460
And yet, one of the things that makes the
Kasha so strange is its very dubious origins.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:41.390
The kanji for Kasha translates to “Fire
Cart” which is why this giant evil cat was

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:45.350
originally depicted as a literal flaming cart.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:49.800
The “hell cart” arose in the Kamakura
period in Japan, a time of great change that

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.180
included the development of several new schools
of Buddhism.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:56.760
Many members of the faith saw the period as
the “end of times” [mappō].

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:02.170
They began producing “hell scrolls” (jigoku-zoshi)—paintings,
often accompanied by text, graphically depicting

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:07.780
the souls of sinners being tortured in various
versions of hell for all eternity.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.629
The illustrations were meant to disgust and
horrify people enough that they would act

00:02:10.629 --> 00:02:15.170
with kindness and humility while they were
alive, so they portrayed things like giant,

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:19.599
fire-breathing roosters, demons chopping up
bodies, and people drowning in a river of

00:02:19.599 --> 00:02:20.760
fiery lava.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:25.879
And some show demons dragging flaming carts
full of damned souls from earth to hell—the

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:27.300
“hell cart.”

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:31.650
Beginning in the Edo period, we continue
to see images of the flaming cart, but now

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:35.920
without the demon, and it’s coming down
from the sky during a storm.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:40.290
Legend had it that if you heard thunder during
a funeral, the Kasha hell cart was coming.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:45.950
Over time, the storm imagery became entwined
with the Kasha, so much so that by the late

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:50.420
17th century, one book (Kii-zodanshu) included
a tale about the flaming cart alongside an

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:54.950
illustration of the Shinto deity of thunder
and lightning, Raijin riding inside

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:55.950
it.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:59.769
So that’s how the storm imagery joined our
understanding of the creature, but how we

00:02:59.769 --> 00:03:03.159
got from a flaming storm cart to a cat is
more ambiguous.

00:03:03.159 --> 00:03:09.000
I mean, Raijin is certainly frightening in
the image, but not really feline in appearance.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.120
The lore and art continued to evolve, the
updated forms depicted Kasha as hairy, catlike

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.450
creatures with long, wing-like ears, smooth
stomachs, and large claws.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:23.420
It was said that during a funeral procession,
the Kasha would suddenly appear, leap upon

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:27.810
the coffin, tear open the lid, and drag the
corpse with it to hell.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:32.230
Kasha are said to have the power to control
the body of a corpse, and even to wake the

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:33.230
dead.

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:37.889
One Kasha story first published in 1837, shows
how the seemingly disparate supernatural elements

00:03:37.889 --> 00:03:40.840
of the Kasha could be collected into one spooky
story.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:45.510
According to the legend, there lies a great
temple in the Echigo province full of old

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:47.129
documents and wondrous treasures.

00:03:47.129 --> 00:03:51.129
One of the most prized possessions in its
collection is a “hellwagon-repelling”

00:03:51.129 --> 00:03:52.549
linen robe.

00:03:52.549 --> 00:03:57.219
Dyed with the sap of the clove tree and splattered
with bloodstains, it is said to have belonged

00:03:57.219 --> 00:03:58.859
to a virtuous Abbot.

00:03:58.859 --> 00:04:03.389
Once a simple linen robe, it became a holy
relic after the Abbot wore it as he accompanied

00:04:03.389 --> 00:04:05.959
a funeral procession through a great snowstorm.

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:10.610
They were halfway to the burial site when
black clouds rolled in, making it as dark

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:11.610
as night.

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:16.109
A colossal fireball appeared out of nowhere
and floated over to the coffin.

00:04:16.109 --> 00:04:20.359
Inside the fireball was a massive, horrifying
hellcat with two tails.

00:04:20.359 --> 00:04:25.400
Spitting, snarling, and hissing, it clawed
at the coffin, trying to get to the body inside.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:30.270
All of the mourners fled—except the Abbot,
who continued his sacred mantras as he lifted

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.939
his holy staff high into the air.

00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:37.080
He struck the Kasha in the head with all his
might, splitting the skull open—spraying

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:41.930
blood everywhere, and splattering his vestment
in hellcat gore.

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:43.430
The monster vanished.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:48.620
Butttt wait, where had the whole cat thing
come from in the first place?

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:52.460
I found that question hard to answer in my
research, so I reached out for some help from

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:56.410
Hiroko Yoda, author of yokai books including
Yokai Attack!

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:58.080
The Japanese Monster Survival Guide.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:03.510
[Hiroko: Yokai, a product of Japan's polytheistic
worldview.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:10.139
Yokai are personifications of natural phenomena,
unexplainable things, or concepts.

00:05:10.139 --> 00:05:15.680
The way people see them has changed a lot
over the course of time.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:23.810
Long, long ago, when bad things happened,
like getting sick or some kind of misfortune,

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:29.110
people explained it as being caused by some
kind of Yokai.

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:32.960
Therefore, people did things to avoid Yokai.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:40.520
But today, Japan is a modern society, so we
treat them as fun characters from folklore.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:49.000
If you ask me about Kasha, the main part of
the story is the fire cart from hell with

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:54.250
the cat essence attached to that as a variation.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:06:02.950
I don't know exactly how or why they became
attached, but there's an old slang term from

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:04.949
long ago.

00:06:04.949 --> 00:06:09.250
. It means red cat.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:17.730
It's a slang for fire because a cat's tongue
is red and it looks like flickering flame.

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:21.789
Fire was a common danger in daily life.

00:06:21.789 --> 00:06:27.830
So cats are common and the fires are common
and that they could be how Kasha became a

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:28.830
fire cat.]

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:33.879
There is one event we can point to that had
a massive impact on how the Kasha was understood

00:06:33.879 --> 00:06:40.300
in Japanese culture—the publication of Toriyama
Sekien’s iconic 1776 yōkai encyclopedia.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:45.699
[Hiroko: He created the first encyclopedia
of Yokai.

00:06:45.699 --> 00:06:53.530
It's called [Kazu Hyakki-yagyou],
which I translated as Japan Demon Illustrated.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:07:00.090
Before that, Yokai had only appeared on the
scrolls with no background.

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:05.419
He made a book and he gave each Yokai background
and context.

00:07:05.419 --> 00:07:08.469
It's the most important Yokai book.]

00:07:08.469 --> 00:07:13.400
This was the first time the yōkai were presented
in stand-alone narratives, effectively setting

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:18.470
the stage for them to increase their popularity
and solidify their individual characters.

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:20.740
Sekien illustrated the kasha as a cat-demon.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:29.629
[Hiroko: Sekien doesn't give much story about
Kasha, but his illustration is very detailed.

00:07:29.629 --> 00:07:36.800
It shows a cat-like creature wreathed in fire,
carrying a human body.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:43.020
What is important to know is that this is
only Sekien’s interpretation.

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:52.360
There are many variations in folklore, but
broadly speaking, the basic story is this.

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:59.389
Kasha is a Yokai that steals dead bodies from
funerals or graveyards.

00:07:59.389 --> 00:08:03.010
In some stories, it's the body of a sinner.

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:06.450
Other times, an innocent victim.

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:08.789
It varies from place to place.]

00:08:08.789 --> 00:08:12.960
Even in all the beautiful weirdness of yōkai,
Kasha are hard to categorize.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:17.650
They aren’t quite bakéneko, a ‘monster
cat’ or ‘goblin cat,’ since the cat

00:08:17.650 --> 00:08:22.080
features are secondary to what actually defines
this creature—how it carries away corpses.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:27.169
We can call them yōkai since they are “monsters”
but they are really more like demons given

00:08:27.169 --> 00:08:31.280
their hellaciousness and the religious implications
of their actions.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:35.810
Cats are ambiguous by nature—they can be
quiet and cuddly one moment, stealthy and

00:08:35.810 --> 00:08:37.389
deadly the next.

00:08:37.389 --> 00:08:42.719
They also toe the line between feral and domestic,
making them easy to perceive as borderland

00:08:42.719 --> 00:08:45.060
creatures in other ways as well.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:47.920
In Japanese folklore, cats are respected and
often feared.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:52.800
So the feline characteristics of the Kasha
could also be explained by the long history

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:54.640
of cat superstitions in Japan.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:09:00.290
[Hiroko: They aren't necessarily, bakeneko,
but there are many superstitions about cats

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:01.290
in Japan.

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:02.779
The west has superstitions, too, like black
cats being witchy or cats having nine lives

00:09:02.779 --> 00:09:03.779
in Japan.

00:09:03.779 --> 00:09:10.400
There's a superstition that if a cat leaps
over a dead body, that the body will come

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:11.459
back to life.

00:09:11.459 --> 00:09:16.180
There are so many sayings related to cats
in Japanese.

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:19.510
They are common pets.

00:09:19.510 --> 00:09:23.880
You can tie a dog up, but you can't tie up
up a cat.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:30.060
Dogs are pack animals who follow owners' orders
to behave, but cats don't.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:32.490
They have independent spirits.

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:39.670
So when you think about that, you can imagine
where the superstitions come from.]

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:43.250
Folklore tradition claims cats can alter their
appearance and bewitch humans.

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:48.769
So as the lore of feline yokai like the Kasha
developed, people began to be more wary of

00:09:48.769 --> 00:09:50.580
household cats.

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:55.100
Some claimed cats would turn into Kasha while
in the presence of a dead body—so domestic

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:57.180
cats would be left outside if a death occurred.

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:02.220
Others said that if a cat lived a very long
life, it would eventually transform into this

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:03.220
yōkai.

00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:08.220
Hiroko told me that in Japan the Kasha is
largely unknown outside of folklore specialists,

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:11.269
in part because most funerals today are cremations.

00:10:11.269 --> 00:10:14.040
The Kasha can’t steal a body that isn’t
there.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:17.310
But that doesn’t mean they are destined
to fade completely into the shadows.

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:25.510
[Hiroko: I grew up with Yokai and I loved
Yokai ever since I was child.

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:30.010
They're scary, but not too scary.

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:31.790
That makes them fun.

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:38.730
Now, that I've grown up, I realized that Yokai's
ability to characterize things, it's a powerful

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:42.240
tool for people in hard times.

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:47.540
Ambiguous in unknown situations make us nervous.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:54.120
Naming them and describing them brings them
under our control.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:59.640
Yokai are the faces that help us process laugh
and move on.

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:04.959
Like today, for example, we struggle with
COVID 19.

00:11:04.959 --> 00:11:10.589
We have vaccines and you feel like we are
kind of starting to control it.

00:11:10.589 --> 00:11:18.370
But in the early month, when it was out of
control, there was so much fear all over the

00:11:18.370 --> 00:11:19.370
world.

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:27.279
Japanese rediscovered an old drawing of a
Yokai from newspaper 240 years ago, that showed

00:11:27.279 --> 00:11:29.250
a Yokai called Amabie.

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:39.680
It said, "There will be abundant harvest,
but if a plague comes, draw me and show people."

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:44.600
People began sharing the drawings on the social
media and it went viral.

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:49.370
Not only in Japan, but all over the world.

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:53.480
Even the Japanese government started using
Amabie on posters.

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:56.290
And now there are so many variations.

00:11:56.290 --> 00:12:02.810
Some say it's a she, some say she is an angel
who cure plague.

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:09.050
There are so many interpretations, but it
made people smile and helped people deal with

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:10.050
their fear.

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:12.830
This is the power of Yokai.

00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:16.459
Probably, that's how it is with Kasha too.]

00:12:16.459 --> 00:12:22.010
Even if they are mainly a form of entertainment
today, yōkai have only increased in popularity

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:25.160
over time, and the Kasha is no exception.

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:28.380
The monstrous cat appears in the long-running
Kitarō cartoon anime.

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:32.310
And is given this new look in a recent yokai-inspired
video game.

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:35.670
Which if I could side eye any harder, I would.

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:38.920
But in many ways that’s the beauty of monsters.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:43.380
One person’s hell cart is another person’s
hell cat—and I think that makes them even

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:45.170
more amazing.

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:48.639
Before you go - if you’re looking for a
fun show for the budding young scientists

00:12:48.639 --> 00:12:53.139
in your lives, we think you would love MEGAWOW
on the PBS Kids Youtube Channel!

00:12:53.139 --> 00:12:57.620
Hosted by comedian Katie Nguyen, this show
is built to get kids excited about science

00:12:57.620 --> 00:12:59.270
through fun and wacky experiments.

00:12:59.270 --> 00:13:02.820
We guarantee your curious scientist will scream…

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:03.820
Megawow.

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:05.470
Tell them Storied sent you!

